A||N||4||glava2876||84 -fuel problems||||1||
Z||000000||glava2876||04-24-2001||07:30 PM||||I'm about 90% sure that my fuel problems are pump or the screen in the tank.To get the other 10% sure I have the following questions; 1) When I take the line off of the fuel filter it doesn't spray all over -is this wrong? When I place the gas line i a jug and crank the engine over,it will push gas into the jug -how hard should it flow and should it stop when I turn the key off? This car sat for 10 years and I estimated that it had about 1/8 tank of old fuel. I added dry gas, injector cleaner and about 6 galons of gas. It ran when I first started it, but wouldn't idle. Replaced the wires, plugs, cap and rotor and it idled fine, but wouldn't rev up at all. It was idling for about 5 min and then stalled and wouldn't start. A splash of gas in the TBI and it fires -so I know the spack is good. Is it possible that the injector cleaner softened up the gum and is plugging the fuel pump screen? Was it a major mistake not removing the tank cleaning it before starting? At this point should I pull the tank and replace the fuel pump? Approximately how much should a pump cost? Thanks to anyone that can get me headed in the right direction. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-24-2001||07:45 PM||||Pinch the RETURN line from the TBI to the tank. See if it starts. This will tell you if the fuel regulator is stuck open.<P>If not, you need to measure the fuel pressure. PITA project on TBI. If you have pressure then the injector may have plugged.<P>At this point you could be looking at a TBI rebuild and a new pump. I'd do both myself. I would not trust that TBI after sitting so long. Not sure I'd trust the pump either.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||glava2876||04-24-2001||09:25 PM||||I tried pinching the line and it still wouldn't fire. I took the top of the TBI off and when I sprayed WD-40 on either side of the fuel regulator it wouldn't pass through, so i don't thing that it is stuck open.If it is stuck shut and my fuel pump is working good, shouldn't the fuel pressure cause spray when I loosen the fuel filter? -it doesn't, that is what made me think that the pump is weak or plugged. Could gumed up fuel plug the screen in the tank? The gas that I get when I turn the engine over doesn't look bad and I don't see any water. Thanks again, Glen||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||04-24-2001||09:32 PM||||Hey Glen!<BR>double check the fuel filter...<BR>It is designed to block water, so it will plug up with a small amount of water...<BR>If you use a 5 gallon fuel can, run a hose from your Fiero to the can and jumper the oil sender to run the pump...<BR>If it pumps well, drain the tank, add 3 pints of isopropyl alcohol (99%) and 5 gallons of fresh fuel and try again...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000004||glava2876||04-24-2001||09:37 PM||||The jumper idea sounds good. Being a new Fiero owner I have to ask the stupid question -how and where do I do the jumpering? Is this on the fire wall? Thanks, glen  ||||reg||1
